  "text": "Rivers shape valleys through the steady movement of flowing water, sediment, and rock. In their upper courses, rivers often travel quickly down steep slopes, cutting narrow V-shaped valleys as they erode the land vertically. Rapids, waterfalls, and potholes may form where resistant rock slows the river’s progress. Farther downstream, the landscape becomes gentler, and the river begins to erode its banks sideways as well as deepen its channel. This creates wider valleys with meanders, floodplains, and terraces. During floods, rivers spread beyond their channels, depositing layers of fertile silt that enrich surrounding soil. Tributaries add water and carve smaller valleys into the landscape, creating branching drainage systems. Over thousands or millions of years, these processes can transform mountains and plateaus into broad lowlands. Although rivers sometimes change course suddenly, their long-term influence is gradual, continuously reshaping the land through erosion, transportation, and deposition.",
